* An array of records, walked by a scan loop, with its fields named.
*
* This is how large BrainFuck programs are actually built: mandelbrot lays its
* tape out as records and navigates them with scan loops, and the array idiom
* in the standard BF library does the same at a smaller stride. A scan stops
* wherever the data says, so the cursor's *cell* cannot be known -- but it
* moved by a whole record each time, so which *field* it is on is exactly what
* it was.
*
* That is what `@field` and `@here` say, and it is the difference between
* naming the parts of a record and counting angle brackets.

@define STRIDE 3

* What it does:
*
*     for record in records:   # a scan walking whole records at a time
*         print(record.letter)
*
@stride STRIDE
@field marker at 0
@field letter at 1
@field digit  at 2

@var head at 0

* Three records, then the zero that stops the scan.
@to head
+{'x'} > +{'A'} > +{'1'} >
+{'x'} > +{'B'} > +{'2'} >
+{'x'} > +{'C'} > +{'3'} >

@to head
@here marker
[
    @to letter
    .
    @to digit
    .
    @to marker
    >{STRIDE}
]
